### What happens with default method conflicts?

When a class implements two interfaces that both define a default method with the same signature, the compiler reports an error and the class must resolve the conflict.

### How does the conflict occur?

```java
package com.bank.compliance;

interface RBICompliant {
    default String generateReport() {
        return "RBI Standard Compliance Report";
    }
}

interface SEBICompliant {
    default String generateReport() { // SAME name and signature!
        return "SEBI Standard Compliance Report";
    }
}

// COMPILE ERROR! "class TradingBank inherits unrelated defaults"
// class TradingBank implements RBICompliant, SEBICompliant { }
```

### How do you resolve the conflict?

```java
package com.bank.compliance;

// Fix 1: own implementation
class TradingBankV1 implements RBICompliant, SEBICompliant {
    @Override
    public String generateReport() {
        return "TradingBank Custom Compliance Report";
    }
}

// Fix 2: delegate to ONE interface
class TradingBankV2 implements RBICompliant, SEBICompliant {
    @Override
    public String generateReport() {
        return RBICompliant.super.generateReport(); // delegates to RBI
    }
}

// Fix 3: combine BOTH
class TradingBankV3 implements RBICompliant, SEBICompliant {
    @Override
    public String generateReport() {
        return RBICompliant.super.generateReport()
            + " | " + SEBICompliant.super.generateReport();
    }
}

public class ConflictDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(new TradingBankV1().generateReport());
        System.out.println(new TradingBankV2().generateReport());
        System.out.println(new TradingBankV3().generateReport());
    }
}
```

```text
TradingBank Custom Compliance Report
RBI Standard Compliance Report
RBI Standard Compliance Report | SEBI Standard Compliance Report
```

### What are the three resolution rules?

| Rule | Description | Example |
|------|-------------|--------|
| Rule 1: Class wins | A class method beats all interface defaults | Class defines generateReport, interface default is ignored |
| Rule 2: Sub-interface wins | More specific interface beats less specific | If B extends A and both have the default, B wins |
| Rule 3: Must override | Two unrelated interfaces conflict | Class must provide an explicit override |

```java
package com.bank.compliance;

interface A { default String name() { return "A"; } }
interface B extends A { default String name() { return "B"; } }

class C implements A, B { }
// NO error! B.name() wins because B extends A (more specific)
// new C().name() returns "B"
```

### What happens when a class defines its own method?

Rule 1 says the class always wins. If a class defines a method that matches a default method signature, the class version takes precedence.

```java
package com.bank.compliance;

interface Auditable {
    default String auditFormat() { return "Standard"; }
}

class BankService implements Auditable {
    public String auditFormat() { return "Custom Bank Format"; }
}
// new BankService().auditFormat() returns "Custom Bank Format"
// The class method wins over the interface default.
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
package com.bank.core;

// WRONG: forgetting to resolve the conflict
// class DualRegulatedBank implements RBICompliant, SEBICompliant { }
// Compile error: inherits unrelated defaults

// WRONG: trying to call super.method() without the interface name
// class BadBank implements RBICompliant, SEBICompliant {
//     public String generateReport() {
//         return super.generateReport(); // WRONG! super refers to class parent
//     }
// }

// CORRECT: use InterfaceName.super.method()
class GoodBank implements RBICompliant, SEBICompliant {
    @Override
    public String generateReport() {
        return RBICompliant.super.generateReport();
    }
}
```

### What about non-conflicting methods?

Methods that do not conflict are inherited normally from each interface with no override needed.

### How is this different from C++ multiple inheritance?

Java's diamond problem is simpler than C++ because interfaces cannot have instance fields or constructors. The conflict is purely about method bodies, not state. Resolution is always explicit through overriding.

### What should a senior engineer know?

The resolution rules are applied in order: class wins first, then sub-interface wins, then must-override. Understanding these rules helps design clean interface hierarchies. The best practice is to keep default methods small and focused on composition (and, andThen) or common patterns, avoiding complex business logic that is likely to conflict.
